This project builds on public reverse-engineering work by others.

1. Termina1/benq-screenbar-halo2-esphome  (MIT)
   https://github.com/Termina1/benq-screenbar-halo2-esphome
   ESPHome bridge in C++. Source of the CRC parameters and the TBCLK
   synchronous-serial requirement. MIT-licensed, so code may be ported with
   attribution; this repo reimplements rather than ports.

2. kuzmin-no/BenQ_ScreenBar_HALO_2_HA_integration  (NO LICENSE)
   https://github.com/kuzmin-no/BenQ_ScreenBar_HALO_2_HA_integration
   MicroPython bridge with the clearest write-up of the frame format, the
   pairing address and the address-discovery procedure.

   No license file is present, so the code is all-rights-reserved and MUST NOT
   be copied. Only the factual protocol description and diagrams from its README
   are used here — facts are not copyrightable. No code from this project has
   been read into this one.

3. zeel01/BenQ-ScreenBar-Halo-2-Investigation
   https://github.com/zeel01/BenQ-ScreenBar-Halo-2-Investigation
   Teardown photographs identifying the Artery AT32F421C8T7 MCU and the
   Holtek BC5602 radio in the remote.

4. Holtek BC5602 datasheet
   https://www.holtek.com/webapi/116711/BC5602v120.pdf
   Modulation, data rates, SPI interface and whitening support.

